#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087437 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087709 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8e2458e5-8061-5f3f-9b4f-4d0bdd0ec641" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Equations Written in Standard Form"}] :question/id #uuid "5faa755c-1e2d-52a7-a521-49f79b94ec39" :question/math-academy-id "e-14709" :question/problem "Consider the equations of the following lines.\n\nI. $-5x + y=3$\n\nII. $3x - y= -2$\n\nIII. $3x= 5$\n\nIV. $2x- 6y= 4$\n\nWhich of these lines is written in standard form?" :question/worked-solution "The *standard form* equation of a line is \n\n$$\nax+by=c,\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $a,b,$ and $c$ are all integers,\n\n- $a\\geq 0,$ and\n\n- $a,b,$ and $c$ have no common factors greater than $1.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our equations.\n\n- Equation I is *not* in standard form because the coefficient of $x$ is negative ($-5$).\n\n- Equation II is in standard form. Indeed, the variables are on the left-hand side, while the constant term is on the right-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{3x- y}_{\\textrm{variables}} \\quad = \\underbrace{\\phantom{y}-2\\phantom{y}}_{\\textrm{constant}}\n$$\n\n \nAll coefficients are integers (namely, $3,$ $-1,$ and $-2$) and don't have common factors greater than $1.$ Also, the coefficient of $x$ is positive ($3$).\n\n- Equation III is in standard form. Indeed, the variables are on the left-hand side, while the constant term is on the right-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{3x}_{\\textrm{variables}} \\quad = \\underbrace{\\phantom{y}5\\phantom{y}}_{\\textrm{constant}}\n$$\n\n \nAll coefficients are integers (namely, $3$ and $5$) and don't have common factors greater than $1.$ Also, the coefficient of $x$ is positive ($3$).\n\n- Equation IV is *not* in standard form because the coefficients $(2,-6,$ and $4)$ have a common factor of $2.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087438 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087710 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f399f6ed-91bc-594e-9a40-b7c79f60c5dc" :knowledge-point/title "Converting from Slope-Intercept Form to Standard Form"}] :question/id #uuid "7a4196bc-1f72-5435-9c00-c1fbef60b549" :question/math-academy-id "e-14710" :question/problem "Write $y = 7x+\\dfrac{2}{5}$ in standard form." :question/worked-solution "Our goal is to write the equation in the form $ax+by = c,$ where $a,b,$ and $c$ are integers with no common factors, and $a\\geq 0.$\n\nWe begin by moving all of the variables to the left-hand side and all of the constants to the right-hand side.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =7x+\\frac{2}{5} \\\\ y-7x & =\\frac{2}{5} \\\\ -7x+y & =\\frac{2}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow the equation is in the form $ax+by=c.$ However, it violates the rule $a \\geq 0.$ We can fix this by multiplying the equation by $-1$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-7x+y & =\\frac{2}{5} \\\\ (-1)\\cdot (-7x+y) & =(-1)\\cdot (\\frac{2}{5}) \\\\ 7x-y & =-\\frac{2}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, $c$ is a fraction, not an integer. To fix this, we multiply by $5$ to get rid of the fraction:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}7x-y & =-\\frac{2}{5} \\\\ 5\\cdot (7x-y) & =5\\cdot (-\\frac{2}{5}) \\\\ 35x-5y & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, $a,b,$ and $c$ are integers with no common factors, and $a\\geq 0.$ So we are done!\n\nThe standard form of the line is $35x-5y=-2.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087439 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087711 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "daa37544-c4ec-5b8b-91a5-ccf5ee8f50d6" :knowledge-point/title "Converting an Arbitrary Line to Standard Form"}] :question/id #uuid "9e0c4000-c24e-5761-8cbe-60699b557eca" :question/math-academy-id "e-14711" :question/problem "Write $y-1=x$ in standard form." :question/worked-solution "Our goal is to write the equation in the form $ax+by = c,$ where $a,b,$ and $c$ are integers with no common factors and $a\\geq 0.$\n\nWe begin by moving all of the variables to the left-hand side and all of the constants to the right-hand side. This gives\n\n$$\n-x+y=1.\n$$\n\nThen, we multiply by $-1$ to make the $x$-coefficient positive:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-x+y & =1 \\\\ (-1)(-x+y) & =-1 \\\\ x-y & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, $a,b,$ and $c$ are integers with no common factors, and $a \\geq 0.$ So we are done!\n\nThe standard form of the line is $x-y=-1.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087440 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087712 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "eefba93e-12c7-5835-80fb-b36463f140d8" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Standard Equation of a Line Passing Through Two Points"}] :question/id #uuid "f78832f7-acd3-5779-b171-0650782b918e" :question/math-academy-id "e-14712" :question/problem "Calculate the equation of the straight line that passes through the points $(-1,3)$ and $(2,4).$ Write the answer in standard form." :question/worked-solution "First, we will construct the point-slope form of the line. Then, we will simplify it into standard form.\n\nTo construct the point-slope form of the line, we need a point and a slope. We already have two points, so we calculate the slope $m$ using the given points:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m=\\frac{{y}_{2}-{y}_{1}}{{x}_{2}-{x}_{1}}=\\frac{4-3}{2-(-1)}=\\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we use this slope along with one of the points to write the point-slope form equation of the line. It doesn't matter which point we choose, but let's choose $(2,4).$ The point-slope equation is as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-4 & =\\frac{1}{3}(x-2)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo simplify the point-slope formula into standard form, we first multiply by $3$ to get rid of the fraction:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-4 & =\\frac{1}{3}(x-2) \\\\ 3\\cdot (y-4) & =3\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}(x-2) \\\\ 3y-12 & =x-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we move the variables to the left side and the constant to the right side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3y-12 & =x-2 \\\\ 3y & =x+10 \\\\ -x+3y & =10\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we multiply by $-1$ to make the $x$-coefficient positive:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-x+3y & =10 \\\\ (-1)\\cdot (-x+3y) & =(-1)\\cdot 10 \\\\ x-3y & =-10\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the standard form of the equation is $x-3y = -10.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186493940 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087710 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f399f6ed-91bc-594e-9a40-b7c79f60c5dc" :knowledge-point/title "Converting from Slope-Intercept Form to Standard Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0cefc6ed-d53f-5610-b027-2f4d4f49fe33" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y-7x=-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186493412} {:answer/id #uuid "491c54a5-c931-5a8c-9a9a-3c88b0327d98" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y+4=-7x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186493514} {:answer/id #uuid "da1160f6-b217-5f4e-8d61-347872e99bb8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7x=-y-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186493732} {:answer/id #uuid "ee1ed2e4-de83-508f-b030-4f5015eeb2bb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y+7x+4=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186493771} {:answer/id #uuid "f556b952-f090-56d3-9d73-f0c358dec808" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7x+y=-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186493787}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f556b952-f090-56d3-9d73-f0c358dec808" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7x+y=-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186493787} :answer-field/id #uuid "e06d190d-4809-59ce-85bf-42f3c5088325" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186493879}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "9b7addf0-160c-54bc-8147-e2a24b57e5bd" :question/math-academy-id "q-109609" :question/problem "Write $y=-7x-4$ in standard form." :question/worked-solution "Our goal is to write the equation in the form $ax+by=c,$ where $a,b,$ and $c$ are integers with no common factors and $a\\ge 0.$\n\nWe begin by moving all of the variables to the left-hand side and all of the constants to the right-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-7x-4 \\\\ 7x+y & =-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, $a,b,$ and $c$ are integers with no common factors, and $a\\ge 0.$ So we are done!\n\nThe standard form of the line is $7x+y=-4.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186493922 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087711 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "daa37544-c4ec-5b8b-91a5-ccf5ee8f50d6" :knowledge-point/title "Converting an Arbitrary Line to Standard Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "06c553f0-8dd8-5f5b-837c-09549d9227e4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=3x-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186493401} {:answer/id #uuid "8206681f-ad95-56cc-9419-2800e67d09c7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3x-y=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186493595} {:answer/id #uuid "8bb7d2ea-8334-5ee0-bae9-43918b279ce9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3x+y=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186493610} {:answer/id #uuid "97591881-5b3c-5e83-beb2-296f55b78078" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x-3y=3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186493629} {:answer/id #uuid "c1c31ec5-c907-5d2c-be84-13eac945c469" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3x-y-1=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186493709}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8206681f-ad95-56cc-9419-2800e67d09c7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3x-y=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186493595} :answer-field/id #uuid "35a19470-a5b0-5f3b-87c6-f21a25237e12" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186493814}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "4c641ffa-5f29-5085-9bd8-14bb6fe11280" :question/math-academy-id "q-109615" :question/problem "Write $3x=y+1$ in standard form." :question/worked-solution "Our goal is to write the equation in the form $ax+by=c,$ where $a,b,$ and $c$ are integers with no common factors and $a\\ge 0.$\n\nWe begin by moving all of the variables to the left-hand side and all of the constants to the right-hand side. This gives\n\n$$\n3x-y=1.\n$$\n\nNow, $a,b,$ and $c$ are integers with no common factors, and $a\\ge 0.$ So we are done!\n\nThe standard form of the line is $3x-y=1.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186493891 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087712 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "eefba93e-12c7-5835-80fb-b36463f140d8" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Standard Equation of a Line Passing Through Two Points"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "37dc7839-3847-5f01-81ef-f713051f0840" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5x+2y=8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186493482} {:answer/id #uuid "864c8f78-6254-5494-8143-e61489b757d5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x-4y+25=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186493602} {:answer/id #uuid "b8f62edd-3019-5453-8498-1e1a4f6a7c08" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2x-3y=-18" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186493693} {:answer/id #uuid "bbbbf3ff-8039-54bc-bfab-f8b2a0aee979" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2x=3y-18" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186493698} {:answer/id #uuid "efe8310a-c429-55cd-8c15-4311cc9072a1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x-4y=-25" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186493776}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "efe8310a-c429-55cd-8c15-4311cc9072a1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x-4y=-25" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186493776} :answer-field/id #uuid "9a41d9d6-ad78-5b1c-ae8a-f0443259b92e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186493852}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "03ad1173-6dbd-512f-86a7-67df49248ff3" :question/math-academy-id "q-109627" :question/problem "What is the equation of the line that passes through the points $(-5,5)$ and $(-1,6)$, expressed in standard form?" :question/worked-solution "First, we will construct the point-slope form of the line. Then, we will simplify it into standard form.\n\nTo construct the point-slope form of the line, we need a point and a slope. We already have two points, so we calculate the slope $m$ using the given points:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m=\\frac{{y}_{2}-{y}_{1}}{{x}_{2}-{x}_{1}}=\\frac{6-5}{-1-(-5)}=\\frac{1}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we use this slope along with one of the points to write the point-slope form equation of the line. It doesn't matter which point we choose, but let's choose $(-5,5).$ The point-slope equation is as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-5 & =\\frac{1}{4}(x-(-5)) \\\\ y-5 & =\\frac{1}{4}(x+5)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo simplify the point-slope formula into standard form, we first multiply by $4$ to get rid of the fraction:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-5 & =\\frac{1}{4}(x+5) \\\\ 4\\cdot (y-5) & =4\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}(x+5) \\\\ 4y-20 & =x+5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we move the variables to the left side and the constant to the right side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4y-20 & =x+5 \\\\ 4y & =x+25 \\\\ -x+4y & =25\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we multiply by $-1$ to make the $x$-coefficient positive:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-x+4y & =25 \\\\ (-1)\\cdot (-x+4y) & =(-1)\\cdot 25 \\\\ x-4y & =-25\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the standard form of the equation is $x-4y=-25.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186493928 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087711 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "daa37544-c4ec-5b8b-91a5-ccf5ee8f50d6" :knowledge-point/title "Converting an Arbitrary Line to Standard Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "378b3a35-e891-5069-bb1c-e487f7301f59" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x+y=-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186493481} {:answer/id #uuid "475cbe15-cd02-5bb4-96cb-090594d20d74" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x+y=-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186493512} {:answer/id #uuid "76d02a01-1789-5535-985c-146ec53f929c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x-y=-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186493568} {:answer/id #uuid "b08504a0-1c63-5fae-b984-a8655b54bb8e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-x+y=-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186493676} {:answer/id #uuid "cade5605-46b0-523d-82c7-ef5ae58a3a9c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x+y=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186493716}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "378b3a35-e891-5069-bb1c-e487f7301f59" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x+y=-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186493481} :answer-field/id #uuid "b236d4f2-5cba-5c8d-9d04-e7159dd85f16" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186493864}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "62452c7f-a9e2-5c51-81ef-baa2650df43c" :question/math-academy-id "q-13339" :question/problem "Write $3y+3x+3=0$ in standard form." :question/worked-solution "Our goal is to write the equation in the form $ax+by=c,$ where $a,b,$ and $c$ are integers with no common factors and $a\\ge 0.$\n\nWe begin by moving all of the variables to the left-hand side and all of the constants to the right-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3y+3x+3 & =0 \\\\ 3x+3y & =-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow the equation is in the form $ax+by=c.$ However, $a,b,$ and $c$ have a common factor of $3.$ So, we divide by $3$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3x+3y & =-3 \\\\ \\frac{3x+3y}{3} & =\\frac{-3}{3} \\\\ x+y & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, $a,b,$ and $c$ are integers with no common factors, and $a\\ge 0.$ So we are done!\n\nThe standard form of the line is $x+y=-1.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088066 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087712 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "eefba93e-12c7-5835-80fb-b36463f140d8" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Standard Equation of a Line Passing Through Two Points"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "592087b3-94fc-571b-8975-e20b1af01fdd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186493530}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "592087b3-94fc-571b-8975-e20b1af01fdd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186493530} :answer-field/id #uuid "4bd3c904-aa13-5663-a037-27214c2b3f20" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186085126} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ccfc196e-d2b9-5e51-b03e-0879a7618102" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3x-y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186493718}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ccfc196e-d2b9-5e51-b03e-0879a7618102" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3x-y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186493718} :answer-field/id #uuid "5ac67115-1932-54f2-8277-c9e7f1701f81" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186085127}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "2b068d36-9b51-5e2a-ba98-4d4ce9e05360" :question/math-academy-id "q-208786" :question/problem "Express the equation of the line that passes through the points $(-3,-2)$ and $(-1,4)$ in standard form.\n\n{{field-1}}\n$=$\n{{field-2}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we will construct the point-slope form of the line. Then, we will simplify it into standard form.\n\nTo construct the point-slope form of the line, we need a point and a slope. We already have two points, so we calculate the slope $m$ using the given points:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m=\\frac{{y}_{2}-{y}_{1}}{{x}_{2}-{x}_{1}}=\\frac{4-(-2)}{-1-(-3)}=\\frac{6}{2}=3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we use this slope along with one of the points to write the point-slope form equation of the line. It doesn't matter which point we choose, but let's choose $(-1,4).$ The point-slope equation is as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-4 & =3(x-(-1)) \\\\ y-4 & =3(x+1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we simplify the point-slope formula into standard form, moving the variables to the left side and the constant to the right side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-4 & =3(x+1) \\\\ y-4 & =3x+3 \\\\ y & =3x+7 \\\\ -3x+y & =7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we multiply by $-1$ to make the $x$-coefficient positive:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-3x+y & =7 \\\\ (-1)\\cdot (-3x+y) & =(-1)\\cdot 7 \\\\ 3x-y & =-7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the standard form of the equation is \n\n$$\n3x-y=-7.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186493934 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087710 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f399f6ed-91bc-594e-9a40-b7c79f60c5dc" :knowledge-point/title "Converting from Slope-Intercept Form to Standard Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bfa1ad90-bf21-5bd7-9c4a-e2c278e3e210" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2x-5y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186493706}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bfa1ad90-bf21-5bd7-9c4a-e2c278e3e210" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2x-5y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186493706} :answer-field/id #uuid "2578c7dc-df07-5c9f-9883-dccf1b4fa276" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186493811} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1900311e-ec2f-5a19-8cfb-a76c9ae262ca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "15" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186493434}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1900311e-ec2f-5a19-8cfb-a76c9ae262ca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "15" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186493434} :answer-field/id #uuid "adee3ac3-8ecd-58c3-a665-8c5d902d4cdf" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186493860}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "8d0eef6f-b433-56d1-a89d-4aab29bca392" :question/math-academy-id "q-214594" :question/problem "Express the equation of the line $y=\\frac{2}{5}x-3$ in standard form.\n\n{{field-1}}\n\n$=$\n{{field-2}}" :question/worked-solution "Our goal is to write the equation in the form $ax+by=c,$ where $a,b,$ and $c$ are integers with no common factors and $a\\ge 0.$\n\nWe begin by moving all of the variables to the left-hand side and all of the constants to the right-hand side. This gives\n\n$$\n-\\frac{2}{5}x+y=-3\n$$\n\nNow, we multiply both sides of the equation by $-1:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{2}{5}x+y & =-3 \\\\ (-1)\\cdot (-\\frac{2}{5}x+y) & =(-1)\\cdot (-3) \\\\ \\frac{2}{5}x-y & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we multiply both sides of the equation by $5:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{5}x-y & =3 \\\\ 5\\cdot (\\frac{2}{5}x-y) & =5\\cdot (3) \\\\ 2x-5y & =15\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, $a,b,$ and $c$ are integers with no common factors, and $a\\ge 0.$ So we are done!\n\nThe standard form of the line is \n\n$$\n2x-5y=15.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186493927 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087709 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8e2458e5-8061-5f3f-9b4f-4d0bdd0ec641" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Equations Written in Standard Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1bbe4881-0890-557a-94a3-38fa413a495b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186493440} {:answer/id #uuid "26f25391-da6a-50f7-9fbb-57467d22e097" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186493453} {:answer/id #uuid "288dfc3c-caee-5e89-b10d-3410df396b1d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186493458} {:answer/id #uuid "319cea52-16bc-509f-b234-b5a50371b20c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186493475} {:answer/id #uuid "9ee14cb3-89bd-5f29-ae17-4f05933c4fde" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186493639}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "26f25391-da6a-50f7-9fbb-57467d22e097" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186493453} :answer-field/id #uuid "867602d4-5dfd-58fb-9675-72a40d72afbb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186493842}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "61b55285-bdfc-5092-a032-79dc8a8f2ea3" :question/math-academy-id "q-216934" :question/problem "Consider the equations of the following lines.\n\nI. $3y=5$\n\nII. $-x+7y=0$\n\nIII. $5x-3y=6$\n\nWhich of these lines is written in standard form?" :question/worked-solution "The *standard form* equation of a line is \n\n$$\nax+by=c,\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $a,b,$ and $c$ are all integers,\n\n- $a\\ge 0,$ and\n\n- $a,b,$ and $c$ have no common factors greater than $1.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our equations.\n\n- Equation I is in standard form. Indeed, the variables are on the left-hand side, while the constant term is on the right-hand side:\n\n$$\n{{3y}_{⏟}}_{\\text{variables}}\\,={{5}_{⏟}}_{\\text{constant}}\n$$\n\n \nAll coefficients are integers (namely, $3$ and $5$) and don't have common factors greater than $1.$ Also, the coefficient of $x$ is non-negative ($0$).\n\n- Equation II is *not* in standard form because the coefficient of $x$ is negative ($-1$).\n\n- Equation III is in standard form. Indeed, the variables are on the left-hand side, while the constant term is on the right-hand side:\n\n$$\n{{5x-3y}_{⏟}}_{\\text{variables}}\\,={{6}_{⏟}}_{\\text{constant}}\n$$\n\n \nAll coefficients are integers (namely, $5,$ $-3,$ and $6$) and don't have common factors greater than $1.$ Also, the coefficient of $x$ is positive ($5$).\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186493955 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087709 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8e2458e5-8061-5f3f-9b4f-4d0bdd0ec641" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Equations Written in Standard Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "77bca3b3-3cec-585b-b9e9-d37688da6161" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of these listed" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186493570} {:answer/id #uuid "8226540d-9c79-5a28-b1a1-75e03dfe607d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186493596} {:answer/id #uuid "b08de677-8ae6-519e-9dd4-3f754943d262" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186493677} {:answer/id #uuid "eea176cd-3d65-5d28-9d12-6524ebb071c2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186493774} {:answer/id #uuid "f1404a4a-a824-58ce-b097-efdd72add08c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186493779}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b08de677-8ae6-519e-9dd4-3f754943d262" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186493677} :answer-field/id #uuid "873a5bb4-ef75-574c-a0d5-064e793e8973" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186493843}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c460c44e-72fe-595b-9fd7-cf8fb6438c3d" :question/math-academy-id "q-216943" :question/problem "Consider the equations of the following lines.\n\nI. $4x+2y=14$\n\nII. $5x=10$\n\nIII. $5x-3y=1$\n\nWhich of these equations are written in standard form?" :question/worked-solution "The *standard form* equation of a line is \n\n$$\nax+by=c,\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $a,b,$ and $c$ are all integers,\n\n- $a\\ge 0,$ and\n\n- $a,b,$ and $c$ have no common factors greater than $1.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our equations in turn.\n\n- Equation I is *not* in standard form because the coefficients $(4,2,$ and $14)$ have a common factor of $2.$\n\n- Equation II is *not* in standard form because the coefficients $(5$ and $10)$ have a common factor of $5.$\n\n- Equation III is in standard form. Indeed, the variables are on the left-hand side, while the constant term is on the right-hand side:\n\n$$\n{{5x-3y}_{⏟}}_{\\text{variables}}\\,={{1}_{⏟}}_{\\text{constant}}\n$$\n\n \nAll coefficients are integers (namely, $5,$ $-3,$ and $1$) and don't have common factors greater than $1.$ Also, the coefficient of $x$ is positive ($5$).\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088219 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087709 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8e2458e5-8061-5f3f-9b4f-4d0bdd0ec641" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Equations Written in Standard Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ef77e145-5232-5daf-8b5f-0fb282cf9be6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186085969} {:answer/id #uuid "31d269e4-a2d4-5b5c-85aa-76e93abcc2d7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186085970} {:answer/id #uuid "3551a33d-6664-58b2-9a9b-881dde1b9a0c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186085971} {:answer/id #uuid "4b0896f6-a2c8-565f-b6c2-08b572b5a62b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186085972} {:answer/id #uuid "6e44c0d4-a88e-511b-a1ea-ce56e6698eb8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186085973}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6e44c0d4-a88e-511b-a1ea-ce56e6698eb8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186085973} :answer-field/id #uuid "7f2db0c7-16fd-5c37-b274-a36499dcdd56" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186085968}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "079b3242-4c32-569f-b103-9bf2414bbafc" :question/math-academy-id "q-59668" :question/problem "Consider the equations of the following lines.\n\nI. $2x=3$\n\nII. $-x+2y=3$\n\nIII. $x-2y=0$\n\nWhich of these lines is written in standard form?" :question/worked-solution "The *standard form* equation of a line is \n\n$$\nax+by=c,\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $a,b,$ and $c$ are all integers,\n\n- $a\\ge 0,$ and\n\n- $a,b,$ and $c$ have no common factors greater than $1.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our equations.\n\n- Equation I is in standard form. Indeed, the variables are on the left-hand side, while the constant term is on the right-hand side:\n\n$$\n{{2x}_{⏟}}_{\\text{variables}}\\,={{3}_{⏟}}_{\\text{constant}}\n$$\n\n \nAll coefficients are integers (namely, $2$ and $3$) and don't have common factors greater than $1.$ Also, the coefficient of $x$ is positive ($2$).\n\n- Equation II is *not* in standard form because the coefficient of $x$ is negative ($-1$).\n\n- Equation III is in standard form. Indeed, the variables are on the left-hand side, while the constant term is on the right-hand side:\n\n$$\n{{x-2y}_{⏟}}_{\\text{variables}}\\,={{0}_{⏟}}_{\\text{constant}}\n$$\n\n \nAll coefficients are integers (namely, $1,$ $-2,$ and $0$) and don't have common factors greater than $1.$ Also, the coefficient of $x$ is positive ($1$).\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""}]}
